        <div class="space-y-4">
          <details class="group bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden" open>
            <summary class="list-none flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 p-6 cursor-pointer hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors">
              Does shaving your back make hair grow back thicker?
              <span class="transition-transform group-open:rotate-180 shrink-0 ml-4">
                <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="h-5 w-5 text-gray-400" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg>
              </span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-gray-600 px-6 pb-6 leading-relaxed">
              No. This is one of the most persistent grooming myths. Shaving creates a blunt tip on the hair, which <em>feels</em> coarser as it emerges, but the actual diameter, color, and growth rate of the follicle remain completely unchanged. Dermatologists have debunked this repeatedly.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
            <summary class="list-none flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 p-6 cursor-pointer hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors">
